From cb4788c49b0880370b68cba50abeb477b72b3367 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jared Wyles <jared.wyles@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 08:42:32 +1100
Subject: [PATCH 33/33] llama: place each request's share of a batch together
 in the KV pool

The unified pool placed one token at a time: a sequence continued after its
highest cell while that stayed inside a 64-cell chunk, and otherwise opened
the next all-empty chunk.

A request that resumes a cached prompt takes over cells that can sit above the
ones it writes next. Its highest cell then never moves, the cell after it is
taken, and every generated token opens a chunk of its own: 128 tokens became
about 128 one-cell runs spread over 128 chunks, each padded to a 32-row block
in the row lists the attention kernels read.

Placement now works per sequence over the whole ubatch
(llama_kv_cells::place_together): the share continues after the sequence's
latest token, wherever that cell is, and the rest goes to the smallest free
stretch that holds it, sized in whole chunks so the sequence can keep growing
there. When no stretch is large enough the longest ones are filled first.

